comments:
I left all three inputs blank. None can be scored YES, and none can be scored NO (0) because I could not show that no such requirement exists anywhere.

1. Planning law read in the Citation Sheet:
- KTCP Act 1961 (ULG-02, PRS compilation with amendments up to Act 38 of 2015). Section 12(1) is a mandatory ('shall include proposals for') list of land-use items: (a) zoning for residential, commercial, industrial, agricultural, recreational, educational and other purposes; (b) street pattern; (c) parks and areas for 'public buildings and institutions'; (d) to (g) future expansion, land reservation, special control areas and phasing. Sections 12(2) and 12(3) cover survey and explanatory reports, zone regulations and heritage. Nothing in s.12 requires a social development plan or a social development component. Public health and affordable housing are not mentioned.
- KTCP Amendment Bill 2025 (ULG-03, as passed by the Legislative Assembly). It does not change s.12. It only adds the Commissionerate (s.2A/2B) and makes GBA the planning authority (s.2(7), s.81-B and s.81-G). It comes into force on a date the government notifies, and that date is not in the sheet.
- GBA Act 2024 (ULG-01). Section 15(1) makes GBA responsible for master plans and mobility plans 'for the infrastructure development'. The section 18 Sectoral Expert Committee on Social Inclusion, Education, Health and Welfare is advisory only. The Bengaluru Metropolitan Planning Committee draft plan has no contents set out.
- BDA Act (ULG-04). Section 16(3) says schemes 'may' provide houses. That is optional and is excluded by the methodology.
- BBMP Act 2020 (ULG-19). Its housing and education items are municipal functions, not required plan contents.
- BMLTA Act (ULG-18). Section 21(2) concerns the Comprehensive Mobility Plan, which is a mobility plan, not a social development plan.

2. Education. The only overlap is mandatory zoning of land for 'educational' purposes under s.12(1)(a). The methodology asks for a mandated social development plan, or a social development component of the plan, that covers education. In my view a land-use zoning category is not that, so I would not credit YES. A person should confirm this reading.

3. Why NO cannot be scored. Several layers are not in the Citation Sheet:
- the KTCP Rules and any rules on Master Plan or zoning regulation contents;
- KTCP amendments after 2015 other than those noted;
- the BMRDA Act 1985;
- rules under the GBA Act;
- any state affordable housing law or rules that tie housing to the master plan.

The rules require that every relevant instrument has been read before a 0 is given. Because these layers were not available to read, absence is not shown, so the result is 'not found' rather than NO for all three components.

4. Please check:
(a) whether the KTCP Rules or zoning regulations require health, education or affordable housing contents in the plan;
(b) whether the methodology credits educational land-use zoning as an Education component;
(c) the date the KTCP Amendment 2025 took effect, and so when GBA became the planning authority.

I checked the Step 2 answer against ULG-05 and agree with it. Two inputs are YES; three are blank as 'not found'.

(d) Built-form codes: YES. The CGDCR 2017 (UD&UHD Notification GH/V/269 of 2017, dated 12.10.2017) says it 'shall apply to the land development and building construction in the entire Gujarat State'. Table 1.1.1 lists the Surat Urban Development Authority (SUDA) in category D1. For D1 areas the CGDCR sets:
- maximum building height by abutting road width (Table 8.3.1: 10/15/25/45/70 m);
- road-side margins by road width (Table 8.4.1);
- a Build-to-Line rule (the line left after the road-side and side margins). This applies only to building units named in Local Area Plans;
- minimum road frontage for fuelling stations (Table 18-1) and signage/billboard controls (Reg. 19).
The method accepts DCRs for (d), so these count.

(e) Universal accessibility: YES. All buildings except Dwelling-1&2 need:
- an entrance accessible to people with disability, with signage;
- ramps no steeper than 1:12, with handrails;
- guiding floor material for visually impaired people;
- non-slip finishes a wheelchair can cross.

Why the CGDCR applies to the Surat Municipal Corporation (SMC): the CGDCR names SUDA, not SMC. ULG-06 (SUDA DP-2035, page 185) says the SUDA area 'consists of Surat Municipal Corporation (SMC) Area and 195 villages', so SMC falls inside the D1 jurisdiction. A reviewer should confirm that the team accepts this state-wide DCR as 'applicable to the city'.

(a) Roads/streetscapes, (b) footpaths and (c) underground utilities: left blank ('not found', not NO).
- No Surat street design guideline, road-hierarchy standard or utility design standard is in the Citation Sheet.
- ULG-07 (Parking Policy 2018) covers parking management, permits, informal public transport (IPT) and freight. It has no street or footpath design standards.
- ULG-03 (GPMC Act) has only powers to set street widths and regular lines, not design standards.
- The CGDCR's internal-road rules and Schedule 26 illustrations are DCR content. The method allows DCR evidence only for (d) and (e), so they do not count for (a).
- The SMC, SUDA, UD&UHD and BRTS/Sitilink websites are not in the Citation Sheet. Absence therefore cannot be shown, and this is a VERIFICATION-LIMIT.

Period: I relied on the CGDCR as notified on 12 October 2017. I did not check later amendments.
